      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 53 year-old-man developed classical angina pectoris, presumably the result of coronary atherosclerosis. Thirty-two years before, an abnormality on a chest film had been described as a \"prominence of the upper portion of the left cardiac border.\" The \"prominence\" was actually an angiosarcoma, which eventually compromised blood flow in the left coronary system."},
